+          "text": "Short answer: yes, there is a clean place, but the gains do not use it, and the code names them only in comments.\n\nWhere extended values already live:\n- In the conversation view (IR), every turn has extra, keyed by harness: Turn.extra[\"claude\"], [\"pi\"], [\"gemini\"]. The codex writer's own docs say foreign namespaces are dropped \"so they would not pollute the JSONL\".\n- In the document, the agent-coding-session kind allows extra properties, and each harness's native leftovers ride in the conversation.append structural change (entry_extra), next to the shared fields.\n\nWhy gains pollute anyway: a writer fills in values the target harness needs, the target's reader reads them back as if the harness had written them, and they land as ordinary fields in that harness's namespace. Nothing marks them as made up.\n\nDo the transformers call them out? Only in comments, not in the data:\n- codex/project.rs module docs: \"For non-Codex sources, the projector synthesizes sensible defaults (originator \"codex-toolpath\", source \"cli\", model_provider \"openai\")\", plus cli_version \"0.0.0-projected\" at line 233. originator is deliberately a marker (\"so Codex-side tooling can tell projected sessions from real ones\"), and nothing reads that marker back.\n- claude/project.rs:81: \"emit a default permission-mode line so Claude Code can resume them\" (\"permissionMode\": \"default\").\n- path-cli/projection/codex.rs:102-105: \"openai\" when the source has no provider.\n\nProposal:\n(1) Every writer records what it synthesized in one place, a toolpath namespace (e.g. extra.toolpath.synthesized: the key paths it filled and why) or a recognisable marker, as codex already does with originator.\n(2) Readers that see toolpath's own marker keep those values out of the shared fields, or flag them.\n(3) The goldens split gains into \"synthesized, declared by the writer\" and \"undeclared\". Only undeclared gains fail, and the check compares values, not just 